# Domains provisioned on this server whose mail still lives somewhere else.
#
# Every domain here exists in this server's virtual_mailbox_domains (it was
# created during the mailcow migration), which normally means "deliver
# locally". But their public MX still points at the old server -- or, for
# customer-12.example, at Zoho. Delivering locally
# would drop the mail into this server's copy of the mailbox, which nobody
# reads, while the real mailbox never receives it. That is exactly what
# happened to mail sent from the webmail to techies.africa.
#
# `smtp:` with no nexthop means "route by the recipient domain's MX", i.e.
# normal internet routing -- so each of these reaches wherever its mail
# actually lives.
#
# REMOVE a domain's line the moment its MX is cut over to
# courier.mailyte.com, otherwise its mail will be relayed straight back out
# instead of being delivered here. customer-14.example is deliberately absent: it is
# already cut over and must deliver locally.
#
# After editing: postfix reload  (no postmap -- see texthash note below)
#
# THIS FILE MUST STAY IN THE REPO. It was originally created directly on the
# server, and a deployment replaced config/mailer/postfix/ with a fresh
# checkout -- deleting it. main.cf still referenced it, so every transport_maps
# lookup failed, and a transport_maps failure makes Postfix reject EVERY sender
# with "451 4.3.0 Temporary lookup failure". All outbound mail stopped.
#
# main.cf uses texthash: rather than hash: so there is no compiled .db to
# generate or to go missing -- Postfix reads this file directly. That also
# means no postmap step: edit, then `postfix reload`.

# 2026-08-27: thirteen domains removed because their MX now points here.
#
# They had been cut over but their lines were left behind, which is the exact
# failure this file's header warns about: Postfix routed each one straight
# back out to its own MX, connected to itself, and bounced the mail
# "5.4.6 mail for <domain> loops back to myself". 167 messages were lost that
# way between 22 and 27 August -- permanently, because 5.x.x bounces are not
# retried. Nothing surfaced it: the sender is told it bounced, the recipient
# never hears, and this server's own queue stays empty.
#
# Only domains whose mail genuinely lives elsewhere belong below. Check the
# MX before adding one back:
#     dig +short MX <domain>
#
# Removed 2026-08-27 (MX -> mail.mailyte.com, deliver locally): customer-01.example,
# customer-02.example, customer-03.example, customer-04.example, customer-05.example, customer-06.example,
# customer-07.example, mailyte.com, customer-09.example, customer-10.example, customer-11.example,
# techies.africa, customer-13.example

# 2026-09-21: customer-12.example removed, and this file now carries no entries.
#
# TWO reasons, and the first is a live bug rather than tidying. customer-12.example's MX
# is now mail.mailyte.com -- it has been cut over -- so the line meant "relay
# this domain's mail to its MX", which is THIS server. That is a loop, and it
# is the exact shape of the 2026-08 incident recorded above: thirteen domains,
# 167 messages, five days, everything reporting healthy throughout.
#
# Second: this is the community repository. A paying customer's domain has no
# business in open-source configuration, whatever it says. The documentation
# above is worth keeping for any operator doing a migration; the customer names
# are not.
#
# The file stays, empty. main.cf points transport_maps here, and a missing
# transport_maps file makes Postfix reject EVERY sender with
# "451 4.3.0 Temporary lookup failure".
